# Dev Notes - le7

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:pythonx, "~> 0.4.2"},
  {:kino_pythonx, "~> 0.1.0"}
])

defmodule MyProcess do
  def wait_for_message(timeout \\ 3000) do
    receive do
      msg -> msg
    after
      timeout -> :timeout
    end
  end

  def loop_forever(), do: loop_forever() 

  defexception [:message]

end

defmodule MyException do
  defexception [:message]
  def exception(_), do: exit(:my_exit)
end 
```

## Processes



<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Processes have mailbox, state, and time on the scheduler.

When a process ends, it can send a signal with the reason it ended

A process can also recieve signals from other processes.  If the signal is an `EXIT` signal then the process will exit, unless `trap_exit` has been set to true

Processes created with Process.spawn:

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
# Process.flag() doesn't matter because we are not monitoring or linking
pid = Process.spawn(fn -> :ok end, [])
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

options can include `:monitor` and `:link`

* `:monitor` will cause a `{:DOWN, pid, _, _, _}` message when the process ends

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, false)
pid = Process.spawn(fn -> exit(:normal) end, [:monitor])
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

* * It doesn't matter what the `:trap_exit` flag is...

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)
pid = Process.spawn(fn -> exit(:normal) end, [:monitor])
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

* * Note: this also causes `spawn` to return a monitor reference with the pid

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
pid
```

* `:link` will cause two processes to be linked together (when one exits, an exit signal is sent to the other).
* * If the exit reason is `:normal` this will be ignored

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, false)

pid = Process.spawn(fn -> exit(:normal) end, [:link])
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

* * if `:trap_exit` flag is true, the exit signal will be changed into a `{:reason, pid, message}` tuple and put in the process's mailbox

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)

pid = Process.spawn(fn -> exit(:normal) end, [:link])
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

## Let it Crash

So far every process has been spawned, executed, and terminated normally.

Processes can also terminate with other reasons or can throw exceptions, causing them to terminate.

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)

pid = Process.spawn(fn -> exit(:stop) end, [:link])
Process.exit(pid, :kill)
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)

{pid,_} = Process.spawn(fn -> MyProcess.loop_forever() end, [:monitor])
Process.exit(pid, :kill)
MyProcess.wait_for_message()
```

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)

pid = Process.spawn(fn -> MyProcess.loop_forever() end, [:link])
Process.exit(pid, :kill)
MyProcess.wait_for_message(5000)
```

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)

pid = Process.spawn(fn -> 0/0 end, [:link])
MyProcess.wait_for_message(5000)
IEx.Helpers.flush()
```

```elixir
Process.flag(:trap_exit, true)

pid = Process.spawn(fn -> raise MyException, message: "my exception" end, [:link])
MyProcess.wait_for_message(5000)
```

## OTP Abstractions

Abstractions like Task, Agent, Genserver, Supervisor were created because some patterns were often repeated

Here are the patterns that have been abstracted:

* Client Section

* * `start_link`
  * `child_spec`
  * API calls
  * Process.send()
  * Process.send_after()

* Server (Callback) Section

* * `init(initial_state)`
  * `handle_xxx(:message, _from, state)`

and for some of the most commonly used abstractions we have these

* Agent

* * API handles get/update and state
  * Agent.get()
  * Agent.update()
  * Agent.stop()

* GenServer

* * API

* * Genserver.call()
  * Genserver.cast()

* * Callbacks

* * handle_call()
  * handle_cast()
  * handle_continue()
  * handle_info()
  * terminate()

* Registry

* * Registry.keys()
  * Registry.lookup()
  * Registry.register()
  * Registry.values()

* *

* Supervisor

* *

* Task

* *

* GenStage (included)

* *

The final value of the callback controls both the return value and how the callback loops to wait for the next message:

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
:ok
|> IO.inspect()
```
